"About us - National Audit Office (https://www.nao.org.uk/about-us/)\nciteturn42view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.nao.org.uk/about-us/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 128\nL0: Search 【0†Search site】\nL1: \nL2: ## Search\nL3: \nL4: Search on NAO [Input] [Input]\nL5: \nL6: Search\nL7: \nL8: 【0†Advanced search】\nL9: \nL10: ### Featured content\nL11: \nL12:   * 【1†NAO recommendations tracker】\nL13:   * 【2†COVID-19 cost tracker】\nL14:   * 【3†Press office】\nL15:   * 【4†Jobs】\nL16: \nL17: ## On this page\nL18: \nL19:   * 【5†Who we are】\nL20:   * 【6†Our impact】\nL21:   * 【7†Our work】\nL22: ## Who we are\nL23: \nL24: The National Audit Office (NAO) is the UK’s independent public spending watchdog.\nL25: \nL26: We support Parliament in holding government to account and we help improve public services through our high-quality audits.\nL27: \nL28: We are independent of government and the civil service. The Comptroller and Auditor General (C&AG), Gareth Davies, leads the NAO. He is an officer of the House of Commons with statutory authority to:\nL29:   * audit and report on the financial accounts of all government departments and other public bodies\nL30:   * examine and report on the value for money of how public money has been spent. ​\nL31: We support the work of the 【8†Public Accounts Committee†committees.parliament.uk】 (PAC). It uses our reports to hold evidence sessions focused on public spending and scrutinising the implementation of government policy. Audited bodies are required to respond to recommendations made by PAC, which are often based on our work.\nL32: \nL33: The 【9†Public Accounts Commission†committees.parliament.uk】 (TPAC), a parliamentary committee of MPs, oversees our work. Read more about the 【10†governance of the NAO】.\nL34: We have a 【11†five-year strategy for 2025 to 2030】. We will focus on helping improve the productivity and resilience of public services and better financial management and reporting in government. By working effectively with Parliament and our other stakeholders, we aim to maximise our impact on trust and value for money.\nL35: ## Our impact\nL36: \nL37: Our work helps Parliament’s scrutiny of government, achieves a positive financial impact and makes a difference to people’s lives. As well as supporting PAC, we also support other select committees to hold different parts of government to account.\nL38: \nL39: Read more about our 【12†support for Parliament】.\nL40: We report on issues that matter to citizens – for example, our work on government’s 【13†progress in achieving net zero】, our reports on the 【14†HS2 programme】, and our report on the 【15†condition of school buildings】.\nL41: \nL42: Our work has a positive financial impact through reduced costs, improved service delivery and other benefits to citizens. Read more about our recent impacts in our 【16†Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26】.\nL43: ## Our work\nL44: \nL45: Our people are experts in a wide range of specialisms. We work together to audit public spending and share our insights to drive improvements in the public sector.\nL46: ### Financial audit\nL47: \nL48: We provide an independent audit opinion on around 400 accounts per year. The C&AG certifies these accounts and reports the results to Parliament.\nL49: \nL50: We audit public sector accounts, including:\nL51: \nL52:   * All government departments\nL53:   * Executive agencies\nL54:   * Arm’s-length bodies\nL55:   * Companies, audited under statute or on a voluntary basis\nL56:   * Some charities\nL57: Our work provides increased transparency that money is spent in line with Parliament’s intention. It gives government bodies the insight they need to manage taxpayers’ money more effectively.​​​\nL58: \nL59: See our latest 【17†financial audit reports】.\nL60: ### Value for money\nL61: \nL62: We report to Parliament on whether government is delivering value for money.\nL63: \nL64: We define good value for money as the optimal use of resources (economy, efficiency and effectiveness) to achieve the intended outcomes.\nL65: \nL66: We publish around 60 value-for-money reports per year. These reports are impactful, timely and relevant and respond to complex challenges facing government. We make 【1†recommendations】 in these reports on how government can achieve value for money and improve services.\nL67: We also publish responsive reports to establish facts where there are concerns about emerging public spending issues.\nL68: \nL69: In delivering our work, our role is not to question government policy objectives. We look at how government has spent money delivering those policies and if that money has been used in the best way to achieve the intended outcome.\nL70: \nL71: See our latest 【18†value for money reports】.\nL72: ### Insights\nL73: \nL74: Our expert 【19†insight teams】 provide valuable and practical insights on how public services can be improved.\nL75: \nL76: We draw these from our extensive work focused on the issues that are a priority for government, where we observe both innovations and recurring issues.\nL77: \nL78: Our insight work includes:\nL79:   * 【20†Lessons learned publications】 – these draw together what we know on cross-cutting issues that matter to government\nL80:   * 【21†Good practice guides】 – helpful overviews that provide practical tips to help stakeholders involved in delivering or overseeing public services\nL81: \nL82: See our latest 【22†insight publications】.\nL83: ### Overviews\nL84: \nL85: We publish factual overviews of government departments to assist Parliamentary select committees in their examination of spending and performance.\nL86: \nL87: The overviews look at the structure, remit, plans and budget of the departments. They summarise the key information and insights that can be gained from our work.\nL88: \nL89: See our latest 【23†overviews】.\nL90: \nL91: ### Other work\nL92: #### International work\nL93: \nL94: Our international work helps us and other audit offices across the world learn from each other to strengthen our efficiency and insight. We have three areas of work:\nL95:   * International relations – we work with other audit institutions and international forums to share experiences and collaborate on global audit issues\nL96:   * International audit – we are the external auditor of some international organisations\nL97:   * Technical cooperation – we help audit institutions in countries that receive UK aid\nL98: #### Code of audit practice\nL99: \nL100: We are responsible for maintaining and publishing the Code of Audit Practice, which is approved by Parliament.\nL101: \nL102: The code sets out what the auditors of local government and health bodies are required to do to fulfil their statutory responsibilities.\nL103: \nL104: Read more about the 【24†code of audit practice】.\nL105: \nL106: \"(Required)\" indicates required fields\nL107: \nL108: Did you find what you were looking for?(Required)\nL109: \nL110: [Input] Yes\nL111: \nL112: [Input] No\nL113: \nL114: How should we improve this page?\nL115: We don’t respond to individual feedback, but you can 【25†contact us】 if you have a question.\nL116: \nL117: Would you like to give feedback?(Required)\nL118: \nL119: [Input] Submit\nL120: \nL121: [Input]\nL122: \nL123: [Input]\nL124: \nL125: [Input]\nL126: \nL127: [Button: Submit] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAbout the Electoral Commission | Elections NZ (https://elections.nz/about/about-the-electoral-commission/)\nciteturn42view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://elections.nz/about/about-the-electoral-commission/\",\"lineno\":null}); Redirected to URL: https://elections.nz/about/about-the-electoral-commission; Total lines: 156\nL0: 【0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Skip to main content】 【2†Skip to navigation within this section】\nL3: \nL4: 2026 General Election 【3†Enrol now†vote.nz】\nL5: \nL6: Menu\nL7: \nL8: 【4†Go to Homepage】\nL9: \nL10:   * 【5†About us】 |\nL11:   * 【6†Media & news】 |\nL12:   * 【7†Jobs†work.elections.nz】 |\nL13:   * 【8†Contact】\nL14: \nL15: Search site\nL16: \nL17: [Input] [Input] [Input]\nL18: \nL19: Search\nL20: \nL21: [Input: I'm looking for ...]\nL22: \nL23: Search Search\nL24: \nL25: Close search\nL26: \nL27: Main\nL28: \nL29:   * Democracy in NZ Te manapori i Aotearoa\nL30: \nL31: 【9†What is New Zealand’s system of government?】\nL32: \nL33: 【10†Political parties in New Zealand】\nL34: 【11†What is an electorate?】\nL35: \nL36: 【12†What is an electoral roll?】\nL37: \nL38: 【13†About elections】\nL39: \nL40: 【14†Past elections】\nL41: \nL42:   * Guidance and rules Ngā tohutohu me ngā ture\nL43: \nL44: 【15†Candidate Hub】\nL45: \nL46: 【16†For parties】\nL47: \nL48: 【17†For starting a political party】\nL49: \nL50: 【18†For third party promoters】\nL51: \nL52: 【19†Advertising and campaigning】\nL53: \nL54: 【20†Donations and loans】\nL55: \nL56: 【21†Election protocols】\nL57: \nL58:   * Getting involved Te whakauru mai\nL59: \nL60: 【22†Teaching voting at schools】\nL61: \nL62: 【23†Educating adults and communities】\nL63: \nL64: 【24†How you can get more involved】\nL65: 【25†Get brochures, posters and forms】\nL66: \nL67: 【26†Download resources】\nL68: \nL69: 【27†Candidate Hub】\nL70: \nL71: 【28†Election Access Fund】\nL72: \nL73: 【29†Community Education Fund】\nL74: \nL75:   * Stats and research Ngā tatauranga me te rangahau\nL76: \nL77: 【30†Enrolment statistics】\nL78: \nL79: 【31†Participation in voting】\nL80: \nL81: 【32†Applications for roll data】\nL82: \nL83: 【33†MMP seat allocation calculator】\nL84: \nL85: 【34†Party donations and loans】\nL86: \nL87: 【35†Election reports】\nL88: \nL89: 【36†Election results†electionresults.govt.nz】\nL90: \nL91: 【5†About us】 【6†Media & news】 【7†Jobs†work.elections.nz】 【8†Contact】\nL92: 【37†Party Portal Login】\nL93: \nL94: [Button: In this section]\nL95: \nL96: About the Electoral Commission Mō Te Kaitiaki Take Kōwhiri\nL97: \nL98:   * 【38†Our leadership】\nL99:   * 【39†Our work】\nL100:   * 【40†Our values】\nL101:   * 【41†Our use of te reo Māori】\nL102:   * 【42†Complaints】\nL103:   * 【43†Corporate publications】\nL104:   * 【44†Report of the Auditor-General】\nL105:   * 【45†Make an OIA request】\nL106:   * 【46†Responses to OIA requests】\nL107:   * 【47†Social media terms of use】\nL108: We’re responsible for running New Zealand’s parliamentary elections and keeping the electoral rolls up to date. We’re an independent Crown Entity which means we work independently from government. We administer the rules in the Electoral Act 1993 for parliamentary elections and the electoral rolls.\nL109: ## Our purpose\nL110: \nL111: We work to provide an effective and impartial electoral system that New Zealanders understand and trust.\nL112: ## Running parliamentary elections\nL113: \nL114: We run every part of parliamentary elections (general elections, by-elections and referendums). Some of our main responsibilities are:\nL115: \nL116:   * registering political parties and their logos\nL117:   * guiding people to comply with electoral law\nL118:   * helping the public to understand how to enrol and vote\nL119:   * allocating funding to parties for election broadcasting\nL120:   * running voting places\nL121:   * counting and processing votes\nL122:   * publishing timely and accurate election results.\nL123: ## Registering voters and maintaining electoral rolls\nL124: \nL125: We register voters and add them to the electoral roll. We keep the electoral rolls up to date and accurate so:\nL126: \nL127:   * all registered voters can take part in parliamentary elections\nL128:   * elections run smoothly.\nL129: \nL130: We also supply electoral rolls to local authorities for use in local authority elections.\nL131: ## Maintaining confidence in the electoral system\nL132: \nL133: To keep confidence in the electoral system, we stay neutral, uphold and follow electoral law and work independently from government. We also advise:\nL134: \nL135:   * political parties, candidates, and third parties on how to comply with electoral rules\nL136:   * the government on proposed or desirable changes to electoral laws.\nL137: ## Helping New Zealanders take part in parliamentary elections\nL138: \nL139: We educate New Zealanders about enrolling and voting. We also work with communities to help them understand and take part in elections, especially communities with low participation rates.\nL140: ## Supporting voters’ rights and protecting their privacy\nL141: \nL142: New Zealanders have the right to decide freely who they will vote for. We make sure that your vote remains secret when you’re voting and when we’re counting votes.\nL143: \nL144: 【1†Back to top】\nL145: \nL146: 【37†Party Portal Login】 【5†About the Electoral Commission】 | 【6†Media & news】 | 【48†Privacy & security】 | 【7†Jobs†work.elections.nz】 | 【8†Contact】\nL147: \nL148: 0800 36 76 56\nL149: 【49†Get in touch via our Facebook†www.facebook.com】 【50†Follow us on Twitter†www.twitter.com】 【51†Follow us on Instagram†www.instagram.com】 【52†Have a look at our YouTube channel†www.youtube.com】 【53†Connect with us on LinkedIn†nz.linkedin.com】\nL150: \nL151: 【4†Electoral Commission】\nL152: \nL153: Copyright ©\nL154: Electoral Commission\nL155: New Zealand\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn42view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.bbc.com/aboutthebbc/governance/mission\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://www.bbc.com/aboutthebbc/governance/mission: (robots.txt) Fetch denied by robots.txt (OAI-SearchBot)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\njudicial review | Wex | US Law | LII / Legal Information Institute (https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/judicial_review)\nciteturn42view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/judicial_review\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 37\nL0: Please help us improve our site!\nL1: \nL2: 【0†No thank you】\nL3: # judicial review\nL4: \nL5: 【1†Judicial】 review is the idea, fundamental to the U.S. system of government, that the actions of the 【2†executive】 and 【3†legislative】 branches of government are subject to review and possible invalidation by the 【4†judiciary】. Judicial review allows the 【5†Supreme Court】 to take an active role in ensuring that the other branches of government abide by the 【6†Constitution】.\nL6: The text of the Constitution does not contain a specific provision for the power of judicial review. Rather, the power to declare laws unconstitutional has been deemed an implied power, derived from 【7†Article III】 and 【8†Article VI】 of the 【9†U.S. Constitution】. Judicial review of the government was established in the landmark decision of 【10†Marbury v.\nL7: Madison†supreme.justia.com】, the first Supreme Court decision to strike down the act of Congress as unconstitutional, with the famous line from Chief Justice John Marshall: \"It is emphatically the duty of the Judicial Department to say what the law is. Those who apply the 【11†rule】 to particular cases must, of necessity, expound and interpret the rule. If two laws conflict with each other, the Court must decide on the operation of each.\"\nL8: While this case has served as the bedrock for judicial review ever since, courts nevertheless must be careful not to violate the 【12†Separation of Powers】 【13†doctrine】 when engaging in judicial review. While of course it is the duty of the judiciary to interpret the law and decide which laws violate the Constitution, judges and justices understand that they must not usurp the legislative duty to create the law.\nL9: While this consideration is often implicit, many judges and justices explicitly rely on it to guide their decision and craft their 【14†opinion】. This principle is also often at the forefront of many important decisions in 【15†administrative law】, where judicial 【16†officials】 must carefully strike the right balance between assessing the validity of executive 【17†agency】 actions without deciding what the law is for themselves.\nL10: [Last reviewed in June of 2023 by the 【18†Wex Definitions Team†about.law.cornell.edu】]\nL11: \nL12: Wex\nL13: \nL14:   * 【19†ACADEMIC TOPICS】\nL15:   * 【20†legal education and writing】\nL16:   * 【21†THE LEGAL PROCESS】\nL17:   * 【22†courts】\nL18:   * 【23†judicial administration】\nL19:   * 【24†legal practice/ethics】\nL20:   * 【25†wex definitions】\nL21:   * 【26†constitutional law】\nL22:   * 【27†courts and procedure】\nL23:   * 【28†legal education and practice】\nL24: ## Wex Toolbox\nL25: \nL26: * * *\nL27: \nL28: * * *\nL29: \nL30:   * 【29†Accessibility】\nL31:   * 【30†About LII】\nL32:   * 【31†Contact us】\nL33:   * 【32†Advertise here】\nL34:   * 【33†Help】\nL35:   * 【34†Terms of use】\nL36:   * 【35†Privacy】\n"
